<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="diffeq-notes-2-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Separable Equations</title>

    <objectives>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Students will be able to...
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <ol>
            <li><p> Identify and solve separable first-order ODEs by separation of variables. </p></li>
        </ol>
    </objectives>

    <definition xml:id="def-separable">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A first-order ODE is <alert>separable</alert> if it can be written in one of the following forms <md> \deriv{y}{x} = X(x)Y(y) \ \text{or} \ X(x)dx = Y(y) dy. </md>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Determine whether EOTF are separable. 
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <md> \deriv{y}{x} = -6xy </md> 
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Notice that <md> \deriv{y}{x} = (-6x) \cdot (y) \ \text{or} \ \frac{1}{y} \, dy = -6x \, dx. </md> So, this is separable.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <md> \deriv{y}{x} = -6x + y </md>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    S.O.L.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </example>

    <example>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Solve the IVP <md> \deriv{y}{x} = -6xy, \ y(0) = 7. </md>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Notice that <md> \deriv{y}{x} = (-6x)(y). </md> So this equation is separable by definition. We first, <alert> separate the variables </alert> <md> \frac{1}{y} dy = -6x \, dx .</md> Integrating both sides, we obtain <md> \ln|y| = -3x^2 + C  \implies y = Ce^{-3x^2}.</md> Applying our initial condition shows that <md> y(0) = C = 7.</md> Therefore, the solution is <md> y(x) = 7e^{-3x^2}.</md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>
</section>
